00:49:08.539 --> 00:49:21.739
And this one, like I remember, yeah, like we were just like tuning out those voices of like head your bets, don't swing for the fences too hard because you're on the bubble.
但对于这部，我记得，是的，我们当时就是把那些让你留有退路、不要把目标定得太高因为你处在被砍边缘的声音全部屏蔽掉了。

00:49:21.739 --> 00:49:30.940
I just felt like, I don't know, and it was foolish and it was a good foolish, I think, because it was just like, I love this show so much.
我当时就觉得，我不知道，这种想法很傻，但我觉得这是一种美好的傻气，因为当时的感觉就是，我太爱这部剧了。

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:32.980
I love the people I'm making this with.
我太爱和我一起创作这些的人了。

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:35.699
Like the world is not going to take this away.
世界绝不会把它夺走。

00:49:35.699 --> 00:49:39.340
of the world, this is going to keep going just because there's so much love here.
从某种宏观角度来说，这部剧会一直拍下去，只因为这里有太多的爱。

00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:42.619
It's so corny, but it's totally like, yeah.
这听起来很老土，但完全就是这样，对。

00:49:42.619 --> 00:49:52.679
And I think that's how we managed to do things like the pineapple and just knowing, I just remember feeling like, we're gonna have years to do the show.
我想这就是为什么我们能拍出像菠萝那样的情节，并且坚信不疑，我只记得当时的感觉是，我们有好几年的时间来拍这部剧。

00:49:52.679 --> 00:49:53.940
I felt it too, I felt it too.
我也是这种感觉，我也是这种感觉。

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:59.280
And I felt like we were, we had turned off the parts of our brain that would let anything but that in.
我觉得我们当时就是，把大脑里任何会产生怀疑的声音都关掉了。

00:49:59.280 --> 00:50:34.289
You have to have this kind of positive, productive delusion that it will keep going and that if you put cool shit out there cool people will come and you'll be able to all make something together and we I think we were we were we were we were basically in that state certainly when we were launching the show we did not let those fears and we didn't overthink it it strikes me that you guys knew just enough to write it in a way that was like recognizable of the form but you had a kind of blissful ignorance in other ways is that allowed you to be, like, become what, bold or weirder?
你必须怀有一种积极的、有建设性的妄想，坚信它会一直拍下去，坚信如果你把酷炫的东西呈现出来，酷炫的人就会被吸引过来，大家就能一起创造出点东西。我想我们当时，当我们推出这部剧时，我们确实处于那种状态，我们没有让那些恐惧占据上风，也没有过度思考。这给我的感觉是，你们懂得刚好够多，所以能以一种熟悉的形式写出来，但在其他方面你们有一种幸福的无知，正是这种无知让你们变得，怎么说，更大胆，更古怪？

00:50:34.289 --> 00:50:35.610
I think bold and weirder.
我觉得是大胆和古怪。

00:50:35.610 --> 00:50:49.489
It's fine, because the thing that jumped into my mind from Jordana's question was, because my son is really into baseball and he's working on hitting and he's been having trouble hitting.
没关系，因为刚才乔安娜的问题让我想到了一个场景，因为我儿子非常喜欢棒球，他正在练习击球，而且他最近击球遇到了困难。

00:50:49.489 --> 00:50:59.090
And it's sort of the advice I've been trying to give him and that his coaches have given him is like- Five shots.
我一直试图给他的建议以及他的教练给他的建议差不多就是——五次机会。

00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:00.769
Yeah, take five shots.
对，试五次。

00:51:00.769 --> 00:51:07.409
No, it's all like, what are you doing with your legs and your, I don't know anything about baseball.
不，全都是关于，你的腿该怎么摆，你的……我对于棒球一窍不通。

00:51:07.409 --> 00:51:11.530
So I'm useless as far as like what the advice is, but there's a lot of advice.
所以在给出具体建议这方面我是毫无用处的，但总有很多建议。

00:51:11.530 --> 00:51:18.090
And like you have like, you can shout from the sidelines, do this, do that, choke up, whatever it is.
就像你可以在场边大喊，这样做，那样做，握短棒，诸如此类的。

00:51:18.090 --> 00:51:28.809
But like it's sort of like, the thing that sort of broke through for him is like, you have to know that you've done enough learning to do this.
但真正让他开窍的那件事是，你必须知道你已经学了足够多的东西来做这件事。

00:51:28.809 --> 00:51:29.494
to do this.
来完成这个动作。

00:51:29.494 --> 00:51:30.490
You're prepared.
你是做好了准备的。

00:51:30.490 --> 00:51:42.941
So when you're actually doing it, don't think about, it's like playing music, playing music is exactly the same thing where it's like you're accessing your body memory as opposed to your brain memory.
所以当你真正去做的时候，不要去想，这就像弹音乐，弹音乐完全是一样的道理，你是凭身体记忆而不是大脑记忆在演奏。

00:51:42.941 --> 00:51:49.416
And I don't sit down when I play, when I play, I don't have to think, okay, a C chord is a C, E and a G.
我坐下来弹奏的时候，我不需要去想，好吧，C和弦就是C、E和G。

00:51:49.416 --> 00:51:50.412
My hand does it.
我的手自己就按上去了。

00:51:50.412 --> 00:51:53.463
And I know what an F is going to sound like next.
而且我知道接下来F和弦听起来会是什么样。

00:51:53.463 --> 00:52:02.490
And that's kind of like when you're up there With the bat in your hand, don't be thinking about like, okay, I gotta bend my knees just like this.
这就有点像当你站在那里，手里拿着球棒时，不要去想，好吧，我得把膝盖弯成这样。

00:52:02.489 --> 00:52:03.329
I gotta do this with my head.
我的头得做这个动作。

00:52:03.329 --> 00:52:06.170
Like you're not, don't think about that because you've already learned that.
别，别去想那些，因为你已经学过了。

00:52:06.170 --> 00:52:08.929
Your body has already learned it, just swing.
你的身体已经记住了，挥棒就行。

00:52:08.929 --> 00:52:10.889
Just do what comes naturally.
顺其自然地做。

00:52:10.889 --> 00:52:13.570
And I feel like that's kind of how we approached the show.
我觉得这就是我们对待这部剧的方式。

00:52:13.570 --> 00:52:16.849
Like you can definitely say, on one hand, we were very inexperienced.
一方面你绝对可以说，我们非常缺乏经验。

00:52:16.849 --> 00:52:19.920
We've never run a show before, but like, I don't know.
我们从来没有制作过剧集，但是，我不知道。

00:52:19.920 --> 00:52:23.199
Like I'd watch 10,000 hours of Charles in charge.
我可是看过一万小时的《查尔斯在掌权》。

00:52:23.199 --> 00:52:24.800
Like I knew how to write a sitcom.
我知道怎么写情景喜剧。

00:52:24.800 --> 00:52:28.519
You know, I knew what a sitcom looked like.
你知道的，我知道情景喜剧长什么样。

00:52:28.519 --> 00:52:30.480
You know, only Charles of charge.
你知道的，只有《查尔斯在掌权》。

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:32.039
I've never watched another show.
我没看过别的剧。

00:52:32.039 --> 00:52:33.559
I've been very careful about that.
我在这方面一直很小心。

00:52:33.559 --> 00:52:39.719
But I do think that some of your youth and experience was an asset.
但我确实认为，你们的年轻和缺乏经验是一种优势。

00:52:39.719 --> 00:52:48.599
Because you were not so rigidly indoctrinated into a system or into a form that you couldn't play jazz with it.
因为你们没有被死板地灌输进某种体制或某种套路里，以至于无法在其中随性即兴地创作。

00:52:48.599 --> 00:52:49.440
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:51.880
It feels like you guys had sitcom was the melody line.
感觉你们就像是用情景喜剧来打底。

00:52:51.880 --> 00:52:53.119
And then you were just playing jazz.
然后你们就在自由地演奏爵士乐。

00:52:53.119 --> 00:52:54.440
You were just like the musician.
你们就像是真正的音乐家。

00:52:54.440 --> 00:53:01.639
Tom Wade said some quote that I won't get right, but I think it was to the effect of like, You don't want to get so good at music that it doesn't surprise you anymore.
汤姆·韦德说过一句我可能记不太清的话，但大意好像是，你可别把音乐学得太精通，以至于它再也无法带给你惊喜。

00:53:01.639 --> 00:53:07.719
Like as a songwriter, you want to not know that much because you want to just be like, well, this sounds good.
作为词曲作者，你不需要懂太多，因为你只想说：嗯，这听起来不错。

00:53:07.719 --> 00:53:08.599
I don't know what this is.
我不知道这是什么。

00:53:08.599 --> 00:53:11.400
Like let's just go find out what that thing is.
就让我们去探索一下那到底是什么吧。

00:53:11.400 --> 00:53:13.239
And you can hear that in great songwriters.
你能在伟大的词曲作者身上听到这种特质。

00:53:13.239 --> 00:53:14.079
It's great.
太棒了。

00:53:14.079 --> 00:53:16.039
Great songwriters aren't always amazing musicians.
伟大的词曲作者并不总是惊艳的音乐家。

00:53:16.039 --> 00:53:16.880
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:53:16.880 --> 00:53:21.880
And we didn't know enough, we didn't know enough.
我们当时懂得不够多，我们真的知道得不多。

00:53:21.880 --> 00:53:22.800
We didn't know all the notes.
我们并不懂所有的音符。

00:53:22.800 --> 00:53:27.480
That's an artist who has refused to uncouple with mystery.
那正是一个拒绝与神秘感分手的艺术家。

00:53:27.480 --> 00:53:36.000
He's like, no, I don't, I want to be, I want to trip into the space of not knowing I want to be surprised and delighted and fascinated and chanted, you know?
他就好像在说：不，我不要，我想进入那种一无所知的状态，我想感到惊喜、愉悦、着迷和陶醉，你知道吗？

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:47.280
And I think, like I said, I just think you guys knew just enough to write it with great professional polish, but you also, you know, you weren't like, like that's not how we do it, you know?
而且我觉得，就像我说的，我觉得你们当时恰好知道得够多，所以能写出高度专业的作品，但你们又不会墨守成规，觉得“我们不能这么干”，对吧？

00:53:47.280 --> 00:53:56.000
We had a meeting once, Jess, we had a meeting like a year or two before we wrote how much mother with some guy, some showrunner guy that had been on lots of staffs and was had a pilot that year.
杰斯，我们曾经开过一次会，大概在我们写《老爸老妈浪漫史》的一两年前，和一个家伙开会，某个当过很多剧组编剧、那年刚好有试播集被看中的制片人。

00:53:56.000 --> 00:53:58.519
And he was just, we didn't love the pilot.
结果我们其实并不是很喜欢那个试播集。

00:53:58.519 --> 00:54:00.000
We had the meeting just trying to get a job.
我们开会纯粹是为了找工作。

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.400
We were like out of work writers.
我们当时是失业的编剧。

00:54:01.400 --> 00:54:04.760
And I remember, if you remember this, but the guy said, we said, oh, great.
我还记得——如果你还记得的话——那个家伙说，我们当时说：“哦，太棒了。”

00:54:04.760 --> 00:54:07.320
You know, you go in there and you compliment the pilot, even if you didn't like it.
你知道的，去开会时总得恭维一下人家的试播集，哪怕你并不喜欢。

00:54:07.320 --> 00:54:08.159
He go, that was so great.
我们就说：“太棒了。”

00:54:08.159 --> 00:54:09.199
We got sent the tape.
“他们把带子发给我们了。”

00:54:09.199 --> 00:54:11.280
We watched it before the meeting is great.
“我们在开会前看了，真的很棒。”

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:14.760
And he goes, yeah, it's kind of like a B minus C plus.
然后他说：“哦，也就差不多B减到C加的水准吧。”

00:54:15.760 --> 00:54:19.039
If this one doesn't go, I'll just write another pilot next year.
“要是这个黄了，我明年再写个试播集呗。”

00:54:19.039 --> 00:54:21.239
And we're like, oh, captain, my captain.
当时我们的反应简直是：哦！长官，我的长官。

00:54:21.239 --> 00:54:24.440
And then you're just in the back of your head.
然后你心里其实在想：

00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:26.350
like, let's never be that guy.
咱们可千万别变成那种人。

00:54:26.350 --> 00:54:33.230
Let's write the one we have to do for fucking decade and we will just die trying because we love it so much.
我们要写那部哪怕要该死地干上十年、赴汤蹈火也在所不惜的作品，因为我们太爱它了。

00:54:33.230 --> 00:54:35.968
And I think that we didn't know any better.
我想，正是因为我们当时初生牛犊不怕虎。

00:54:35.968 --> 00:54:39.153
We didn't think there was another pilot next year.
我们才不觉得明年还会有另一个试播集。

00:54:39.153 --> 00:54:39.918
This was it.
这就是全部了。

00:54:39.918 --> 00:54:45.586
I was listening to a podcast with James Hollis, who's a great Jungian analyst and writer.
我当时在听一个播客，嘉宾是詹姆斯·霍利斯，一位伟大的荣格学派分析师和作家。

00:54:45.586 --> 00:54:50.173
And it wasn't his quote, but the name of the podcast that he references.
这不是他的原话，但播客里提到的那个名字。

00:54:50.173 --> 00:54:57.179
It's something like, I might have been Bukowski or something, but it's find what you love and let it kill you.
大概是，可能是布考斯基或者别的什么人说的，但大意是：寻找你所爱的东西，并让它毁灭你。

00:54:57.179 --> 00:54:58.326
It's a great line.
这是一句很棒的话。

00:54:58.326 --> 00:55:00.173
Well, that's that was a show.
好吧，那这就是那部剧。

00:55:00.173 --> 00:55:04.504
Yeah, I'm Joja name check me and I just want to say this about that.
对啊，乔·加（Joja）点名了我，我只想对此说：

00:55:04.504 --> 00:55:05.141
It's Josh.
我是乔什。

00:55:05.141 --> 00:55:09.600
I'm a little stunned that she has just seemed right deep into my soul.
我有点震惊她刚才简直直接说到了我的灵魂深处。

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:12.339
I don't know what you've told her about me.
我不知道你跟她聊过我什么。

00:55:12.339 --> 00:55:18.199
But yes, the difficulty in letting go is like literally what I talked to my therapist about.
不过是的，那种难以放下的感觉，简直就是我跟心理医生谈论的话题。

00:55:18.199 --> 00:55:22.212
And since I just had to cancel my therapy session for tomorrow.
而且鉴于我刚刚才取消了明天的一场心理咨询。

00:55:22.212 --> 00:55:24.759
Literally, I'm glad she brought this up.
真的，我很高兴她把这个提出来。

00:55:24.759 --> 00:55:25.460
This is me.
这简直就是在说我。

00:55:25.460 --> 00:55:34.059
But my answer is, interestingly, exactly like all of yours, my version of letting go is doing something artistic sort of freely, right?
但有趣的是，我的答案跟你们所有人完全一样，我所谓的“放下”就是某种随性而为的艺术创作，对吧？

00:55:34.059 --> 00:55:39.919
Like the day up until you get to shoot or whatever it is, that's all the brain stuff, right?
比如，直到要开拍之前的那些日子，全都是关乎大脑思考的事，对吧？

00:55:39.919 --> 00:55:44.760
That's all the structure stuff and the check again, the right and the wrong.
全都是关于结构、反复确认什么是对的、什么是错的。

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:50.811
And then for me, it's like, when I get a chance to be on set directing, then that's letting go.
然后对盘我来说，就像是：当我终于有机会在片场当导演时，那就是一种放下。

00:55:50.811 --> 00:55:59.728
That's like, oh, we just finally get to do this thing and that has this sort of feeling of freedom and that's what I'm hearing from you too.
就好像是，哦，我们终于可以开干了，那种感觉就像是获得了某种自由，这也是我从你身上听到的感受。

00:55:59.728 --> 00:56:09.346
And I mean, for Ted, it's a little more just, he's just kind of out there, he's getting drunk and he's letting himself do things he would otherwise do.
我的意思是，对泰德来说，稍微更直接一点，他就是放飞自我，喝得酩酊大醉，让自己去做平时不会做的事。

00:56:09.346 --> 00:56:14.952
But the letting go so that you can create is definitely the inspirational moment for me.
但为了创作而选择放手，对我来说绝对是灵感的迸发点。

00:56:14.952 --> 00:56:58.520
I think that's that's really a stewed Alec because I and I think that that's like I feel like it's reminding me of like back in the days when like I guess when like when like the Appetel movies started coming out and like you know it was it was that style of making tv and film that where there's a lot of improv like we sort of like come up with the sort of we were like there's not really a skip we're just gonna or let or like curb enthusiasm and I remember a lot of of writers really like bristling against that where it's like, oh, so like a joke that an actor comes up with on a set in the moment is going to be better than this joke that we worked on in the room for two weeks.
我觉得亚历克这个总结太精辟了，因为我觉得这让我想起了过去那些日子，也就是阿帕图（Apatow）电影开始流行的时候，你知道的，那种拍电视剧和电影的风格包含了很多即兴创作，我们就好像是想出某种——我们当时想，其实并没有剧本，我们要么就——或者像《抑制热情》，我记得很多编剧当时对此非常反感，觉得：哦，所以一个演员在片场即兴想出来的笑话，会比我们在编剧室里苦思冥想两周写出来的笑话还要好吗？

00:56:58.519 --> 00:57:08.920
And it's kind of like in a lot of cases, yeah, because like you're sitting in a room working on it for two weeks as opposed to just like being in the moment and like reacting to the moment.
然后事实往往在很多情况下还真是这样，因为你是坐在房间里苦想两周，而不是身处当下、对当下做出即兴反应。

00:57:08.920 --> 00:57:27.299
And I don't know that so much of good stuff comes out of that part of my reaction when was talking about as being or watching myself is that I think, I mean it's 20 years later, I just feel like a better actor now because 20 years, 20 years more of doing the thing, you'll get better at it.
我不知道，很多好东西其实都源于这种即兴。我当时的一大反应，或者说如今回看自己当时的表现时，我觉得——毕竟已经是20年后的今天了——我现在感觉自己是个更好的演员了，因为多经历了20年的磨炼，你自然会变得更好。

00:57:27.299 --> 00:57:38.199
But I think one of my issues as a younger actor was really wanting to do it right, which is like, that's a, that's a illusory goal, like there's no, there's not such thing.
但我认为我年轻时当演员的一个问题，就是太想把事情做“对”了，而这其实是一个虚幻的目标，根本没有这种标准答案。

00:57:38.199 --> 00:57:40.519
Yeah, but you don't know that at that time.
对，但你当时并不懂这些。

00:57:40.519 --> 00:57:54.559
I was trying to be good and trying to do it right and be funny, but I wish, especially in the drunken stuff, even though I was almost like playing at an arctic but in a more controlled way.
我当时只想表现得好，想把动作做对，想搞笑，但我在拍那些喝醉的戏时——尽管我当时其实是在模仿一个酒鬼，但用的是一种更有控制力的方式——

00:57:54.559 --> 00:57:59.519
I wish I had surprised myself more is what I'm saying.
我真希望当时能给自己更多惊喜，这就是我想说的。

00:57:59.519 --> 00:58:05.960
Having worked now with Al Pacino, that guy is not trying to get it right.
现在和阿尔·帕西诺合作过后我发现，那家伙才不是在试图把戏演对。

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:09.840
He's just trying to get a moment of truth that the camera catches.
他只是在努力捕捉一个能被镜头捕捉到的真实瞬间。

00:58:09.840 --> 00:58:12.300
he's willing to be like, do all sorts of weird shit.
他愿意去尝试各种各样古怪的烂活。

00:58:12.300 --> 00:58:14.460
So that happens, you know?
好让那种真实自然发生，你知道吗？

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:33.059
And I just feel like a little bit more like I understand the magic of on camera acting, which is you're just trying to live life under, you know, you're in a imaginary truthful under imaginary circumstances, as they say, but like, you're trying to catch lightning in a bottle.
所以我觉得我稍微更能理解镜头前表演的魔力了，那就是：正如他们所说，你只是在虚构的情境下努力体验真实的生活，但你是在试图“瓶中捉鳖”。

00:58:33.059 --> 00:58:40.219
You're trying to catch something where you're like, I hope the cameras were rolling on that one because that felt real, you know, that felt true.
你是在试图捕捉某种时刻，让你觉得：“我希望刚才开机了，因为那一刻感觉太真实了，感觉太对了。”

00:58:40.219 --> 00:58:45.500
